mmunicate with the cloud  CDN server the next feature we want to  talk about is custom origins prior to  the release of custom origins the only  way for content to be fulfilled on cloud  CDN is through infrastructure or through  content that's actually hosted and  domiciled on Google Cloud whether that  be a storage instance or a compute  instance with the introduction of custom  origins you now can attach third-party  origins that are not part of your Google  Cloud tenon in order to have that be  delivered by cloud CDN so this now gives  you the ability to extend the life of  your existing origin infrastructure and  still support cloud CDN this also can be  useful as part of a migration strategy  as you move content into Google Cloud  you can use cloud CDN as a bridge to  kind of optic eight your origin  infrastructure migrate your  infrastructure into Google Cloud and  then change the pointer of the back end  so this becomes a huge enabler for not  only cloud CDN but also as part of a  content my dream migration strategy the  other thing to kind of point out here as  a differentiator is with the custom  origins we support not only HTTP HTTP  1.1 but we also support HTTP 2 so while  the end user client to the cloud CDN  edge can support HTTP 2 many other  content delivery providers downgrade the  protocol from the edge talking back to  an origin with custom origins you have  the ability to maintain the HTTP  protocol from edge all the way to the  origin so you can take advantage of  head-of-line blocking and multiplexing  capabilities of the HTTP  you protocol from an integration  perspective something to be cognizant of  is with these third-party origins they  still must be publicly available so you  can't use an RFC 1918 private IP address  for this it still must be a publicly  available IP address subsequently you  also that web server also needs to be an  HTTP oriented web server whether it's  HTTP or HTTPS in terms of its protocol  exposure so you can't use an