 to fulfill that  specific edge request the next feature I  want to highlight here is cloud armor  we've recently now enabled cloud armor  to work with the cloud CDN origin  infrastructure for those of you that are  not familiar with cloud armor cloud  armor is Google's in line security  offering for waffe and network layer  protection and this is designed to help  protect origin compute resources  specifically with within cloud armor you  it supports OS mod security rules but it  also supports IP whitelisting  blacklisting as well as country level IP  whitelisting blacklisted the benefit of  using both cloud armor ends and the CDN  platform together is it gives you  fantastic volumetric protection but it  also provides presentation layer  protection such that you can now protect  origin workloads and deflect requests  that have been detected by the security  rules or your IP or country list  blocking schema so  your origins don't need to deal with  those requests that you know that we  know are basically of malicious intent  the other benefit of working with cloud  armor and the cloud CDN platform  together is that the messaging for cloud  armor is directly embedded within Google  load balancer log lines so with both of  these solutions in place you have a  single source of truth where you have  visibility in terms of what is allowed  or denied and if we denied or allowed a  request you can see the policy rule from  cloud armor that specifically was being  enforced in order to affect that  specific log line request now we'd like  to turn into a little bit into the  second half of the year  what are we intending to bring to market  within cloud CDN the first feature I  want to highlight is flexible cache  control with inflexible cache control  there are three core capabilities that  fall under this umbrella  there's cache control there's header  manipulation and there's error handling  as we talk about cache control before  the flexible cache control release cache  control header values need to be  dictated by your origin web server and  many customers have complained that that  was not how they wanted to control the  cache setup with the flexible cache  control release the the cloud CDN  platform now will have the ability to  apply cache control rules on the CDN  platform itself this will help improve  the cache offload rates by you no longer  having to manipulate your origin web  server but it also simplifies the  workflow process where the CDN  automatically will apply the rules  rather than you having to be more  specific about your work and path and  header manipulation within your web  server configuration specifically from  an integration standpoint we will have  three  main setting options so we will still  maintain origin cache control values and  that still will be available to you but  we're going to add two new capabilities  for being able to serve cached objects  for static objects as well as the  ability to just simply cache everything  where in this case we will ignore  whatever decoration the origin is  supplying in terms of cache control  header values whether it's private  public etc and we'll just apply our own  TTL setting values based on your  configuration setting in the case we're  using cached static objects mode of  operation what we will do is we will  apply the cache control values that you  dictate in your CDN configuration based  on specific mind types and those mime  types are listed here on our slide the  third component or the second component  we want to talk about with inflexible  cache control is header manipulation and  within header manipulation there are a  couple of different use cases that will  be addressed with this release the first  one is the ability to add downstream  response headers to the end-user client  this is helpful for things like adding  course header values which can be  important for for streamed objects or  JavaScript objects that require  additional helper module like