sted  itself in reduced of site availability  we're making modifications to the way  that cloud CDN behaves to to enhance  your site availability the first  extension of that is with negative  caching so specifically if you advertise  certain HTTP status codes from your  origin we have the ability to actually  cache those requests to help buffer your  origin from these responses the second  extension specifically around error  handling is the ability to serve stale  content so by default when an end-user  request is made to the clouds  and edge we're going to make the  determination if we have that content  we'll serve it if we don't have that  content but we have the object sitting  in cash we're going to consult with the  origin for revalidation of that object  of whether or not we can continue to  serve that out of our cash in the event  that the origin isn't available we would  in the past serve up an error response  with this new capability we now have the  ability to serve that stale content  rather than serving up the error page so  this is going to again help increase  your website availability but it also  helps buffer your origin at times of  high load from an integration  perspective some helpful tips to kind of  kind of share with you so first off by  default Google will be turning on  serving of stale content by default the  the TTL default value is one day so in  the event where we have an object in our  edge cache and we can't revalidate it  with the origin the default posture will  be to serve out stale rather than  serving up an error message subsequently  the negative caching will be an opt-in  feature where it will be available to  you but you must elect to configure and  turn that behavior on in your  configuration set that negative caching  will not be enabled by default in our  next that our next level of enhancements  that I would like to discuss with you is  around platform enhancements and the  first feature we'll want to highlight is  resou