control header values need to be  dictated by your origin web server and  many customers have complained that that  was not how they wanted to control the  cache setup with the flexible cache  control release the the cloud CDN  platform now will have the ability to  apply cache control rules on the CDN  platform itself this will help improve  the cache offload rates by you no longer  having to manipulate your origin web  server but it also simplifies the  workflow process where the CDN  automatically will apply the rules  rather than you having to be more  specific about your work and path and  header manipulation within your web  server configuration specifically from  an integration standpoint we will have  three  main setting options so we will still  maintain origin cache control values and  that still will be available to you but  we're going to add two new capabilities  for being able to serve cached objects  for static objects as well as the  ability to just simply cache everything  where in this case we will ignore  whatever decoration the origin is  supplying in terms of cache control  header values whether it's private  public etc and we'll just apply our own  TTL setting values based on your  configuration setting in the case we're  using cached static objects mode of  operation what we will do is we will  apply the cache control values that you  dictate in your CDN configuration based  on specific mind types and those mime  types are listed here on our slide the  third component or the second component  we want to talk about with inflexible  cache control is header manipulation and  within header manipulation there are a  couple of different use cases that will  be addressed with this release the first  one is the ability to add downstream  response headers to the end-user client  this is helpful for things like adding  course header values which can be  important for for streamed objects or  JavaScript objects that require  additional helper module like helper  directives to allow the browser to  leverage the particular object that is  being delivered the next capability  within the header manipulation bucket is  the ability to bypass a cache on a  header request and this can be helpful  to distinguish between an authorized and  an unauthorized user or it can be used  to determine things like a logged in or  a logged in user so for example you  might want to have a cached page  experience for for unauthenticated users  and in the case where you have a more  personalized experience and the user  actually is authenticated you might want  to bypass the cache and go directly to  the origin for a more customized  experience delivered up from the origin  itself  the third use case under the header  manipulation is the ability to restrict  the methods that are being used so by  default Google is going to allow all the  met all different method types to get  funneled into your origin web server  with this new capability out of the  flexible cache control we now have the  ability to be more prescriptive in terms  of what feature mod mod method types  that we want to allow into the origin  the benefit here is that this will allow  you to tighten up your security policy  so for example if your website doesn't  accept post requests then we can  specifically deny post requests on the  CDN edge rather than funneling those  requests all the way back to the origin  where potentially it could be right for  vulnerability or exploitation the other  part of this when we talk about things  like adding course headers it helps  enrich the client request experience so  that we can take advantage of  capabilities that are native inside the  browser such as cores capabilities the  third extension of the flexible cache  control is error handling prior to the  release of flexible cache control and  the error handling module the cloud CDN  platform would pass through whatever  error it was handed to it from the  origin and this kind of manifested  itself in reduced of site availability  we're making modifications to the way  that cloud CDN behaves to to enhance  your site availability the first  extension of that is with negative  caching so specifically if you advertise  certain HTTP status codes from your  origin we have the ability to actually  cache those requests to help buffer your  origin from these responses the second  extension specifically around error  handling is the ability to serve stale  content so by default when an end-user  request is made to the clouds  and edge we're going to make the  determination if we have that content  we'll serve it if we don't have that  content but we have the object sitting  in cash we're going to consult with the  origin for revalidation of that object  of whether or not we can continue to  serve that out of our cash in the event  that the origin isn't available we would  in the past serve up an error response  with this new capability we now have the  ability to serve that stale content  rather than serving up the error page so  this is going to again help increase  your website availability but it also  helps buffer your origin at times of  high load from an integration  perspective some helpful tips to kind of  kind of share with you so first off by  default Google will be turning on  serving of stale content by default the  the TTL default value is one day so in  the event where we have an object in our  edge cache and we can't revalidate it  with the origin the default posture will  be to serve out stale rather than  serving up an error message subsequently  the negative caching will be an opt-in  feature where it will be available to  you but you must elect to configure and  turn that behavior on in your  configuration set that negative caching  will not be enabled by default in our  next that our next level of enhancements  that I would like to discuss with you is  around platform enhancements and the  first feature we'll want to highlight is  resource coalescing with resource  coalescing this helps particularly with  things like live streaming when we have  lots of requests all looking for the  same object in the past each request  would be treated individually with  resource coalescing or sometimes called  request collapsing we have the ability  to have to fulfill that request from  multiple edge requests from a single  request and share out that  response to the edge server rather than  making lots of individual requests  this helps with content offload from the  origin and also helps with content  scaling for high-volume de vents the  next platform enhancement that I want to  talk to you about is TCP enhancements so  as part of our recurring kind of theme  of always improving our time to first  byte and also improving our throughput  response rates we are making  modifications to the way that cloud CDN  will interact with the end user client  all of this is invisible to the end-user  all of this is invisible to you from a  configuration standpoint it just  magically will get better specifically  on the TCP enhancements we're making  modifications to our throughput but  we're also making enhancements to our  pacing so today we're using pacing from  web that leverages bbr for example one  of the things that we're looking at  adding into the platform in the second  semester is looking at adding the bbr  version to again another another  advancement coming from Google the next  item that I want to talk to you about is  HTTP 3 so today we support quick it's  more specifically G quick and this can  be found already embedded and it's  already enabled on Chrome and and latest  Firefox browsers today and this is now  enabled on your platform that you can  enable and opt into in your cloud CDN  configuration but we're not stopping  there we are now going to be extending  the quic platform from G quick and we're  transforming that to I quick which is  now the new standard for the quic  protocol the I quick is kind of a  precursor of sorts to the HTTP 3  protocol and as we start to roll out I  quick and the HTTP 3 protocol to the  entire cloud CDN fleet cloud CDN  customers will automatically have the  ability to leverage the  new protocol the benefit of using the  HTTP 3 protocol is going to improve not  only your time to first byte but also  improve the client performance and  stability for end-user clients  particularly on unstable networks so in  summary the cloud CDN platform is making  a lot of investment in our platform not  only in terms of enhancing and adding  new features and functionality to the  cloud CDN platform but we're also  looking at extending our  industry-leading performance  characteristics and capabilities  likewise were also making additional  investments in cloud CDN to revamp the  user experience so it's going to become  easier and more enriching environment  for you to use cloud CDN without having  to go to multiple different screens and  with that I want to thank you for  attending my presentation today and I  welcome to see you on a future  presentation
 

